<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Qi (206 BC)</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 203 BCE · capital Linzi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The revived kingdom of Qi in Shandong, fought over by the Tian clan, Xiang Yu and Han Xin during the Chu-Han war, until Han Xin made himself its king in 203 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> split the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a> into three, making the Qi general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-du">Tian Du</a> King of Qi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-rong">Tian Rong</a>, the clan strongman left out of the settlement, drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Du out within months, killed the kings of Jiaodong and Jibei, and made himself king of a reunited Qi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Qi became the main distraction that kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> from crushing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>. Xiang Yu invaded in 205 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-rong">Tian Rong</a> was killed, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-heng">Tian Heng</a> rallied resistance and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-guang">Tian Guang</a> as king, with himself as chancellor. The kingdom rested on the loyalty of the Tian clan and the wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 203 BCE, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang's</a> envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yiji">Li Yiji</a> negotiated Qi's submission, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a> attacked anyway, routed the relief army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-ju">Long Ju</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river-shandong">Wei River</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-guang">Tian Guang</a>. Han Xin became King of Qi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-heng">Tian Heng</a> later fled to an island; summoned by Liu Bang in 202 BCE, he killed himself rather than submit, and his followers on the island did the same.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qi-206-bc.jpg" alt="Qi (206 BC)" width="320" height="134"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qi_3_forms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 203 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Linzi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tian Du, Tian Rong, Tian Guang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qi (Tian Du), Qi (Tian Rong)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-204&amp;focus=qi-206-bc" aria-label="Qi (206 BC) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7605 835 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qi (206 BC) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7925 1091L7739 1235M7925 1091L7963 1617"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7739 1235h0M7963 1617h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7925 1091h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7925 1091h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Linzi. <a href="/atlas?year=-204&amp;focus=qi-206-bc">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

